ion The commissioner said the decline in external support highlighted the need for the Somali government and public to strengthen self-reliance and contribute jointly to relief efforts.Organised by SoDMA and running on 25–26 October, the meeting brings together members of the diaspora, religious leaders, businesspeople and representatives of humanitarian organisations. Discussions focus on strengthening community solidarity, coordinating relief efforts and improving systems to prevent and respond to El Niño-related risks. Moallim said the two-day meeting would provide an opportunity to discuss how the government and different sections of society could work together on these priorities. More articles for you Kalonzo: Dualling Nairobi-Mombasa Road Is Priority If Opposition Wins 2027 Polls Somaliland Rejects Somalia-Issued BSP Tickets for Its Airport Departures Ad Ad Like ( 0 ) Save ( 0 ) Share More Articles Back to Top Related Articles Suspect Nabbed for Spending Sh6.7M Mistakenly Credited to His Account Oct 25, 2026 News Read more → PS Omollo Asks Sugar Farmers to Repay Ruto’s Reforms With 2027 Votes Oct 25, 2026 News Read more → PS Raymond Omollo Advises ODM to Trust Ruto, Avoid Formal Pre-Election Pact Oct 25, 2026 News Read more →",
      "summary": "Humanitarian aid to Somalia has plummeted by 70% over the last two years, raising concerns that it could cease entirely, according to Mahmoud Moallim Abdulle, the commissioner of the Somali Disaster Management Agency (SoDMA). Speaking at a meeting in Mogadishu, he emphasized the urgent need for the Somali government and citizens to enhance self-reliance and collaboratively contribute to relief efforts. The meeting, organized by SoDMA, aims to address the risks associated with El Niño and involves various stakeholders, including diaspora members, religious leaders, and humanitarian organizations. Discussions will focus on fostering community solidarity, coordinating relief efforts, and improving systems to mitigate El Niño-related challenges. Moallim highlighted that the two-day event serves as a platform for exploring how different sectors of society can work together to tackle these pressing issues. The situation underscores the critical need for sustained support and proactive measures to address the humanitarian crisis in Somalia.",
